A||||9||Taijiguy||Muncie 5 speed w/ 2.8?||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||07-08-2000||03:33 PM||||I haven't seen much talk about a Muncie 5 speed here. I did a search, but only found one reference to this trans.<BR>I found a Muncie 5 speed at my friendly u-pull-it yard, sitting in a cradle that looked suspiciously like the Fiero (it was front wheel drive though) Anyway, the ID on the trans was ONNX, and the numbers were 61ONNXM000598 I have no clue what this is out of, although I do know it came from an X body, as they keep them grouped at this yard. Anyone have any idea as whether this will hook to the 2.8? I checked the input shaft, and it was 1x14, which gave me hope. It shifts great, and turns nice, and I can grab it for probably less than 100 bucks if it'll work. Any input? <P>Thanks all,<P>Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||07-08-2000||05:00 PM||||Aren't you referring to the Isuzu 5sp that was coupled with 4cyl models?  If so, that tranny is pretty sweet.  A friend of mine just installed on behind his 2.8 from an '88 4cyl coupe.  It really shifts nice.  Almost feels like a Honda clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||07-08-2000||06:44 PM||||If there are three bolts along the top edge of the gearbox, it's an Isuzu. Also, the Getrag as two 3in. circular covers in the side.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-08-2000||07:25 PM||||OK, I know that the Getrag was in reality a Muncie trans. I know the one I found wasn't out of a Fiero, although it looks exactly the same. I found a sticker on the trans that clearly states it's a Muncie. My primary question was if the trans for the Fiero was really unique, or if it was a standard design trans, meaning, is it possible this trans will fit into the Fiero cradle and bolt up to the 2.8 without modification or use of an adaptor plate.  This trans really looked exactly like the one in the cradel on the floor of the garage, and I've been thinking how nice it would be to have a five speed.....<BR>Oh yeah, even the shift levers look exactly like the ones on my GT (minus the brackets,) the starter opening is even on the correct side. The slave cylinder however fits into an opening in the bell housing, and not on a bracket like the four speed, I don't know if the "Getrag" used this system or the same setup as the four speed.<BR>Still seeking more enlightenment about the possibilities, before I throw a hundred bucks at this tranny.<P>Thanks boys and girls,<P>-Taiji<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 07-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick's Dad||07-08-2000||10:46 PM||||Hmmm... If I read my 22P correctly....<P>The Muncie/Getrag 282 5speed was used in a few cars other than the Fiero. It's latest iteration (The 284) is currently available in the Cavalier/Sunfire, the Grand Am, and the Alero, I think, all with the base engines.<P>It seems from GM's Fiero book that the 282 has a bracket holding the slave cylinder in place. This piece shouldn't be too tough to find, whether from the same boneyard or from Kick Hill or other used parts suppliers.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||RLMay||07-08-2000||11:33 PM||||I suggest you check out Rodney Dickman's site.  He provides some information on the Getrag transmission which was used in some GM front wheel dirve cars.  It does require a modification to the shift linkage on the transmission.  He also makes some parts for the conversion.  The slave cylinder setup on the 5-speed is similar to the 4-speed, but it does use a different slave cylinder.  If the clutch fork shaft lever is not external to the bell housing I would be suspecious that it is not the correct transmission.||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||07-09-2000||12:18 AM||||The Getrag is really built by Muncie. GM just calls them "Getrag" to distingush it from the Muncie 4 spd.<P>Rodney has excellent information on using a non-Fiero Getrag in a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodney||07-09-2000||09:38 AM||||This is the "other" Getrag from a FWD. The slave bolts directly to the bellhousing and the releae arm is in the bellhousing not outside like the Fiero. A few have used this Getrag in a Fiero. Watch for trans mounting holes. Some may be different. I have yet to find a good answer on the slave to use. It needs to be a smaller ID. And it has to be determined the correct mounting with the correct lenght of the push rod. But you can switch the case half to make it a correct Fiero bellhousing type. Internally they are all the same.<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||07-09-2000||10:36 AM||||THANKS RODNEY! Whatta guy!<P>Any advice on the shift cable mounting? Or will the bell housing swap accomodate that, and allow use of the old mounting. Do you happen to have a five speed shifter layin around for sale? Anyone......?<P>I'll post all the info I get on this after I do the install, in case anyone else wants to do this swap. I'll research the trans and find out what it's out of exactly.<P>Thanks again, you da man.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 07-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||07-09-2000||06:16 PM||||Keith Huff and I (formerly from Fiero Motorsports)had an interesting situation that should be enlightening to this very topic.<P>We had a F-40 kitcar that had origanaly wanted to have a 3.4 put in. Afterwards we were putting everything back together including a new slave cylinder. When we were finished we were dismayed that the clutch arm was further back than normal. We check to see if we could adjust the arm on the rod any further to take up the slack but it proved to be unsuccessful. Though we didn't want to do it we went back in and took the tranny out to see if the bearing had shifted away from the arms. We had a stock clutch fork available and were beginning to wonder if the tranny came from a Fiero or from the clones. After we took down the tranny we took out the clutch fork and sat for a good 20 minuts looking and comparing the 2 forks. Our conclusion: After pulling out the mirometers and various other measuring props we found that the fork on one was in a different position then stock by a little more than a quarter inch. This translated to the half inch difference we had in the clutch arm. When we installed the clutch arm and reassembled everything the clutch arm was right where it was supposed to be. <BR>    We could have added an extension or tapped on more grooves into the arm for further adjustment but we were both to afraid of making a bad situation worse and just the thought of us adding on more Mickey Mouse mechanics to this already butchered kitcar (we refused to work on the wiring other than engine electronics.)would have left a bad taste in our mouths. So, the conclusion:<P>Same tranny in differnt cars but maybe different clutch arm! Its hard to tell the difference when its in the junkyard!||||reg||2
